Brave Search as the default, with alternatives one click away (Phase 4)

privacy/README.md has claimed a Brave Search default since Phase 4 was
written, and privacy/tracker-controls/kavacha.js says "wired via search config"
-- but nothing was wired. The shipped config's globalDefault was "google", and
a build would have searched with Google while the marketing said otherwise.
This closes that gap, which is the cheapest open item on the v1.0 MVP
checklist and the most visible.

Upstream does not carry the engines we need. In the pinned dump:

  brave      absent
  kagi       absent
  startpage  present but gated behind variants:[{environment:{experiment:...}}]
             so it is never available to an ordinary profile
  ddg        present
  google     present, and the global default

Zen's scripts/update_service_dumps.py could only REMOVE records, so there was
no mechanism to express any of this. It gains two operations:

  add.records   append whole records; a record already present under the same
                identifier is replaced, so the merge is idempotent and can
                also serve as a full override
  patch         override named top-level fields on records already in the
                dump, matched by identifier and/or recordType

patch exists so we do not have to restate records we only partly disagree
with. Startpage needs its availability widened and nothing else -- its name,
URLs and partnerCode should keep tracking Mozilla. Copying the whole record to
change one field would silently freeze the rest at today's values.

A patch rule that matches nothing is a hard build failure, not a warning. The
failure mode it guards against is specific: upstream renames or restructures a
record, our intent stops applying, and the build ships with the default engine
quietly back to Google. That symptom is invisible until someone opens the
browser and types a query, which is far too late.

Config changes:

  * brave and kagi added as full engine records, allRegionsAndLocales.
  * startpage's variants widened to allRegionsAndLocales.
  * defaultEngines.globalDefault set to "brave".
  * defaultEngines.specificDefaults CLEARED. Upstream uses it to hand whole
    regions and distributions to other engines -- zh-CN to Baidu, several ISP
    distributions to their own -- which would have overridden the privacy
    default for precisely the users least positioned to notice or change it.
    A default that applies only in some countries is not a default.

Neither new record carries a partnerCode. Upstream engine records carry
affiliate codes that identify the distribution to the search provider on every
single query. Kavacha takes no revenue share and sends no such identifier, so
the field is omitted entirely and {partnerCode} never appears in a URL.

Verified by running the merge and validating every resulting record against
Firefox's OWN schema (toolkit/components/search/schema/search-config-v2-
schema.json) with jsonschema 4.25.1:

  54 engine records, 0 schema errors
  brave / kagi / startpage / ddg / google all present
  globalDefault "brave", specificDefaults []
  startpage variants widened, search base still upstream's
  perplexity still removed (Zen's own removal preserved)
  re-running the merge is a no-op -- byte-identical output, stable count

The merge operations were also unit-tested directly: add appends, re-adding
the same identifier does not duplicate, patch sets only the named fields, an
unmatched rule exits, and a rule anchored on neither identifier nor recordType
is rejected rather than matching everything.

The "one click" half ships as a Search groupbox in the Privacy Center pane,
next to the claim it qualifies rather than three panes away: a menulist of
every visible engine, following the live default. It sets the default with
CHANGE_REASON.USER, which matters -- patch 0003 watches for a user-initiated
change and adopts it into the active Space's override, so a switch made here
survives the next workspace switch instead of being reverted by it. The pane
observes browser-search-engine-modified so it stays honest when the engine is
changed from the search bar, about:preferences#search, or a Space override.

Not verified on a real build: that Brave is the engine a fresh profile
actually searches with. That needs an import plus a build, and then a query.
The config is proven correct against the schema; the runtime is not proven.
Worth pairing with the R3 network-silence test, since both are assertions
about what a fresh idle profile talks to.
